# refusal-known-failures.allow — ratchet ledger for the B6/B8 refusal lanes.
# Companion: regress/run-refusal-compat-docker.sh. Precedent:
# regress/known-failures.allow, contrib-known-failures.allow,
# plpgsql-known-failures.allow.
#
# Semantics are the other ledgers': a listed check may fail without failing CI;
# an unlisted check that fails breaks the build; a listed check that starts
# passing is stale and also breaks it. The file can only shrink.
#
# SEED: 2026-09-05 against main @ e5971f6b2c. 10 passed, 1 failed.
#
# What these lanes assert, and why the ledger is short: when pgrust cannot do
# something it must say so LOUDLY. Every refusal is also checked for not being
# a Rust panic -- in a thread-per-backend server an unported path that panics
# is a crash, not a refusal.
#
# Passing today, recorded so a regression is visible: CREATE EXTENSION on
# dict_int, xml2 and passwordcheck each errors with an actionable message and
# leaves the server alive; LOAD of a missing library errors; a data directory
# whose PG_VERSION is an older (17) or newer (19) major is refused with
# "database files are incompatible with server"; a truncated pg_control is a
# clean FATAL rather than a panic.

# --- genuine defect ---------------------------------------------------------

# archive_library accepts the name of a library pgrust cannot load, without
# complaint, so archiving silently never happens. This is the mechanism behind
# contrib-known-failures.allow's basic_archive entry, which that ledger
# describes as failing "SILENTLY (no LOAD error), so the runner's not-ported
# detector cannot see it" -- this check is that observation made executable.
#
# It is the most dangerous shape of defect this project has found: a
# configuration that looks accepted, a backup story that looks configured, and
# no archived WAL. Closing it means rejecting an unloadable archive_library at
# ALTER SYSTEM / startup the way C rejects an unloadable shared library.
archive_library-refused
